WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:03.870
GREG BRAIL: Hello.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.010
Welcome.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.510
My name is Greg Brail.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.880
I'm here also with
Keith Danekind.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.120
And together, we're going to
talk about APIs and how people

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.960
use APIs to create connected
digital experiences to create

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.780
new lines of business,
new product areas,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.152
new things out of APIs, and
out of all kinds of technology.

00:00:22.152 --> 00:00:24.360
We're going to start out
with some examples about how

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.120
people actually have used
APIs to solve real business

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.230
problems.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.630
Then we're going to talk
a little bit about what

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.820
this means by showing you
a demo of what it means

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.350
to be a consumer of an API.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.785
Then we'll go into some more
detail about the technology.

00:00:36.785 --> 00:00:38.160
And after we do
that, we're going

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.160
to have another demo,
which is going to show you

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.030
how you actually
use Apigee in order

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.850
to actually create and
manage these APIs programs.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.890
And finally, we'll have time
at the end for questions.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.700
So if you do have questions, I
know there's ways in the tool

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.830
that you can actually do
that while we're talking.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.000
And we'll be getting
to those at the end,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
so don't be surprised if we
don't address them right away.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.490
So I wanted to
start off by talking

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.350
about interesting
things that businesses

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.800
have done around the world,
primarily using APIs.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.990
And there are some really
interesting aspects here.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.020
So for instance, if
we look at Starbucks.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.020
They put in their annual report
that 13% of their revenue

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.640
comes through mobile apps.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.580
Those mobile apps are not built
on completely new technology

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:18.990
infrastructure
that never existed

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.250
before because Starbucks has
a big infrastructure already.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.570
Instead, what they
did is they used APIs.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.760
And the mobile
apps talk to APIs.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.620
And the APIs then
give the mobile apps

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.900
just what they need to
give a really good user

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.600
experience to those apps
based on Starbucks' existing

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.165
technology.

00:01:35.165 --> 00:01:36.540
Other examples,
for instance, are

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.970
Swisscom, which has
actually been offering

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.910
APIs as a service for some of
their network infrastructure

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:42.660
in Switzerland.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.150
Or Arity, which is part
of the insurance business.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.080
It used to be, in order to bring
a business partner on board,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.280
they would have a three
to four month process

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.720
of negotiation, emailing,
Microsoft Word docs,

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.230
all that kind of fun stuff.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.500
Now with APIs, they allow
their business partners

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.410
to get on board with their
business-to-business services

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.680
within a few days, completely
within self-service.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.180
And we'll talk a little bit
more about why that's important,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.720
and why that's a big part
of everyone's API programs.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.972
The way people use APIs
and use API management--

00:02:12.972 --> 00:02:15.180
because this talk is also
going to be a lot about API

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.358
management and what it means--

00:02:17.358 --> 00:02:18.900
is we've been doing
this for a while.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.660
I've been part of
Apigee since 2007.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.430
We weren't talking
about APIs back then,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.320
but we started soon after.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.710
And a lot of customers who come
into this world of APIs and API

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:32.670
management, they start off
with a point to point problem.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.810
My goodness, we need to launch
an app for iPhone and Android.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.360
And we don't have any
technology in our existing

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.920
system that can
actually talk over

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.330
the internet to those devices.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.950
How am I actually
going to connect

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.800
these mobile devices and
all these kinds of things

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.660
to my legacy systems?

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.730
And that's where
we started to see,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.760
five or six years ago,
people doing basic web APIs

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.870
with API management.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.100
Using API keys in order to
protect access to an existing

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.650
service, implementing
things like OAuth,

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.360
putting things like
quotas in place

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.543
in order to protect
access to the internet.

00:03:05.543 --> 00:03:07.710
But where things have gotten
really more interesting

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.270
recently, and the
companies that have

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.460
had the most interesting
successes with APIs,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.350
are people who are
creating differentiating

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.520
digital experiences.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.550
That is, people are creating
new ways of doing business,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.687
not only through mobile
apps, but through things

00:03:19.687 --> 00:03:22.020
like digital assistants, which
we see all over the place

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:22.590
nowadays.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.360
And the real fun
comes when people

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.790
are able to actually transform
their business models

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.620
and create new
ecosystems and turn

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.230
their companies into platforms
that other people can

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.220
do business on.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.400
And I'm going to talk about
a few examples of those.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.428
A great example here is Magalu.

00:03:37.428 --> 00:03:38.970
If you're not
familiar, they actually

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.770
used to be called
Magazine Luiza.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.540
They're the largest
retailer in Brazil.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.280
And they have been
a brick and mortar

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.030
retailer for a very long time.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:48.100
And what they've done
over the last few years

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.420
is they started to change
into more of a platform

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.360
that third parties use
to do commerce in Brazil.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.550
They obviously have a
huge infrastructure.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.650
They have a huge
physical infrastructure.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.980
And they started off with
that basic task of creating

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.090
new digital experiences.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.470
Hey, we need mobile apps.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.600
We want to be able
to offer e-commerce.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.150
But it quickly pivoted
into actually, now

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.940
they have about--
well, about 100 APIs.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.610
But they have thousands
of partners and suppliers

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.560
who use their
platform to do things

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.050
like sell products using
their infrastructure,

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.269
ship using their supply chain.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:25.170
Very much like Amazon
in the US became

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.860
not just an online store,
but a platform for e-commerce

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.170
that thousands and
probably even more,

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.080
producers and consumers
use to transact business.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.570
Magalu is doing the
same thing in Brazil.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.490
They're sort of becoming
the Amazon of Brazil,

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.300
which is ironic because Brazil
already had one of those.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.430
But now they have
a digital one too.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.080
Another great example
is Accuweather.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.300
Everyone knows Accuweather
because some of us

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.750
still watch TV sometimes, and
our weather information-- also,

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.360
our weather information on our
various mobile apps and things

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.280
and on the web-- comes
from Accuweather.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.170
They have a
well-deserved reputation

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.410
for giving excellent
weather forecasts.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.500
They process about $50
billion API requests a day.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.120
And the vast
majority of them are

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.010
from the weather
app on your phone

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.920
or your watch or your
refrigerator or whatever,

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:10.610
right?

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.350
But what Accuweather
also does is

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.540
they have some high
volume forecast products

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.820
because long-term
weather forecasting is

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.060
very important in a
lot of industries.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.730
Financial services, oil
and gas, agriculture, these

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:26.120
depend greatly on long-term,
very accurate weather

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:26.810
forecasts.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:28.710
That's what Accuweather
is known for.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:31.190
And those forecast products
are pretty valuable.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:33.410
So Accuweather has
whole tiers of APIs.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.750
And they have ways in order
to bring business partners

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.030
on board with those
higher value APIs

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.910
to actually charge them the
value of that interesting data

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:42.800
that they're giving
them, and also

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.410
to keep track of
who's using the API.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.655
And one more example
is Ticketmaster.

00:05:47.655 --> 00:05:49.280
We all know Ticketmaster
because that's

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.090
where you go when you
want to buy a ticket.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.090
They, of course, have a
mobile app and a website

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.860
and all those kinds of things.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.590
But they also-- one thing
that not everyone knows--

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.110
is Ticketmaster
actually offers APIs

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.080
that allow third parties
to transact business

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.330
and to buy tickets
on Ticketmaster.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.420
And in 2016, actually, which
was a little while back,

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.897
but 10% of their ticket sales
came from those partners.

00:06:10.897 --> 00:06:12.980
And some of those partners
are things like Costco.

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.397
You can buy tickets at Costco.

00:06:14.397 --> 00:06:16.730
But there are other aspects
where people have integrated

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.200
Ticketmaster into apps.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.715
So for instance, you just
bought plane tickets.

00:06:20.715 --> 00:06:22.340
So where would you
like to buy tickets?

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.720
You bought plane
tickets to New York.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.910
Do you want to go see "Harry
Potter and the Cursed Child"

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:26.702
while you're there?

00:06:26.702 --> 00:06:29.070
A travel app for a travel
site, for instance,

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:31.940
can now use the Ticketmaster
API to integrate that directly

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.310
into their app without
jumping out to a completely

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:36.060
different digital experience.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.287
So what kind of holds
all these together?

00:06:38.287 --> 00:06:40.370
It's that all these various
companies are not only

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.530
using APIs to solve
business problems,

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:46.490
but they're not looking APIs
purely as a bit of technology.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:49.670
They're looking at
the API as something

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:50.960
that is for a customer.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:52.538
They're looking at
it as a product.

00:06:52.538 --> 00:06:54.830
And I think that's where
things get really interesting.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.770
And that's where APIs get really
successful is when you don't

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.320
just think of it is, how are
we going to get the technology

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.320
to get the JSON to the mainframe
that's going to look at the XML

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:04.550
and talk to the SAP?

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.400
But how are we actually
going to create something

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.470
that developers are
going to want to use.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.500
So for instance, Magalu
deals with retail partners.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.970
So their API product managers--
and big successful APIs

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.180
have product managers-- have
to think about questions like,

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.490
what kind of technology
do these retailers use?

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.350
What data formats
can they handle?

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.120
How are they connected
to the internet?

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.370
What sort of things do
they need to do in order

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.170
to actually access these APIs?

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.890
Similarly, Accuweather,
Ticketmaster, and we've

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:34.550
all been there.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.190
Most of us are developers
or we've developed software

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:39.110
and we know what we
like and we don't like.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.527
And what I've always said about
developers for a long time

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:44.540
is that developers aren't lazy,
but developers are in a hurry.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.472
And they have a
very, very fast way

00:07:46.472 --> 00:07:47.930
of telling by
looking at something,

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:49.850
is this something I
want to engage with?

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:51.800
Is this something I want
to spend my time on?

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.360
Or is this something
that's completely off

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.910
in a different direction and
is going to be a waste of time

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:58.850
or is going to require me to
pull in a bunch of technology

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.338
that I don't actually need?

00:08:00.338 --> 00:08:01.880
The great thing
about APIs is that it

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:06.770
was based on a very, very simple
technology base of HTTP, JSON,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:10.520
OAuth, TLS, technologies that
are already prevalent anywhere.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.980
So that a developer who
wants to learn to do an API

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.800
doesn't need to install
a giant tech stack,

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.470
but can actually get started
right away using technology

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:20.120
they probably already know.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:22.490
So what we've seen between
projects and products

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:23.840
is that it's really about--

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.840
even looking at things
like what is your revenue?

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.730
What is your CSAT for your API?

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.370
What are your lead
generation capabilities?

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:34.190
Is there someone who is looking
every week at how many people

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:35.990
have signed up to use the API?

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.960
How has API traffic
changed over time?

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.880
Have new people started
to come on board?

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.710
Have people who have
been customers before

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:43.958
stopped using the API?

00:08:43.958 --> 00:08:45.500
There are a lot of
interesting things

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:46.583
that have to happen there.

00:08:46.583 --> 00:08:49.153
It's just like running
any other digital product.

00:08:49.153 --> 00:08:50.570
And with that said,
we're actually

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.820
going to start
with a demo of what

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.400
it looks like to be
a consumer of an API.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.590
What does it look like
to discover an API,

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:00.830
look at the documentation
for the API, sign up the API,

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:01.680
and get started.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.542
So Keith is going to show us
his demo system right now.

00:09:04.542 --> 00:09:05.750
KEITH DANEKIND: Thanks, Greg.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:06.080
OK.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.040
So for the demo,
we're going to look

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.457
at this from three different
perspectives; the application

00:09:09.457 --> 00:09:12.350
developer, the API
developer, and the API team.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.665
So the application
developer or API consumer

00:09:15.665 --> 00:09:17.540
might be saying things
like, all I want to do

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:18.560
is build an application.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.770
Make it easy for me to
consume your services.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:25.730
The API developer, who's taking
services and packaging them up

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:27.830
so that that application
developer can use them,

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:29.330
that person might
be saying, I need

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:30.900
to share a service
with other people.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:31.900
But there's some limits.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.500
We have to control
what they can do.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.930
The API team who's
running the API program,

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.430
they may be saying
something like, I

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.800
want to think about API
as a software products.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.560
There's customers, there's
life cycles, there's versions.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.953
I want to measure my program
and I want to tune it.

00:09:44.953 --> 00:09:46.370
So in this case,
I'm going to play

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:49.850
the role of the application
developer or API consumer.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.070
And in this case,
one of our suppliers

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:53.810
has a large ERP system.

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:55.520
And we place orders
with them, and we'd

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.353
like to check on the
status of those orders.

00:09:57.353 --> 00:10:00.520
And this is a somewhat time
consuming process right now.

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:03.440
They've granted us an API
to be able to go speed up

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.440
that process and automate
this, and to integrate

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.860
that information into our
internal tracking applications.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.400
So let's see what it looks like
to use the Order Management

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.980
and Order Tracking APIs.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:16.695
So here's their
developer portal.

00:10:16.695 --> 00:10:18.070
And what I want
to learn about is

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.528
how to track the
status of an order

00:10:19.528 --> 00:10:21.080
if I have an order number.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.480
So let's see what
they have here.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:23.410
Get Started.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:25.360
Get a key and test out the APIs.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.190
Learn about and try our APIs.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.260
So well, how do you get started?

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:30.620
Get started in 3 steps.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:33.370
Sign in, register apps,
access the API keys.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:33.880
OK.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.750
That sounds easy enough.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:38.700
Let's see what the APIs are.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:42.120
Looks like there is a
order status tracker here,

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.100
and someone's written
some nice documentation

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:45.600
to make this easy for me.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.110
So I call a URL, I put an order
number at the end of there.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.050
I'm going to need to supply an
API key and an order number,

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.720
and I should be able
to make this work.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.830
I'll get back some data
that looks like this.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:58.320
So that's pretty good.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:01.260
I have a reasonable idea
of how this might work.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.280
I'm going to go
register for an API key,

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.580
and I'm going to test this out.

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:09.510
So I come over to here, and
I'm going to click on Apps.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:11.527
I'm going to register
a new application.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:28.520
And we use this
order tracking API.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:30.770
When I create this, it'll
register my application

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:34.982
and it'll give me an API key.

00:11:34.982 --> 00:11:36.440
So now that we have
that key, let's

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:38.870
go back to the documentation
and try it out.

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:48.230
I'll put the key in there.

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.790
We'll grab a sample
order number.

00:11:55.203 --> 00:11:55.870
And there we go.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:57.287
That's what the
results look like.

00:11:57.287 --> 00:11:59.320
So I see the data is
in here, and that's

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:03.440
what I would expect to be
returned when I call this API.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:04.760
Easy enough to do.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.150
Let's build an application.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.420
So here's a simple order
status tracking application.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:15.380
You put an order number
in here, press search,

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.820
and we get back some
status of this order.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.880
So we get all the details there.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.640
Very easy to write this, took
me about 15 minutes or so.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:24.920
And best thing
was, I didn't need

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:28.280
to have any knowledge of what's
on the back end system, how

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.110
this order system on
the other end works.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:33.200
I just had a simple API to call
in and it returned that data.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.080
I built a single page web
application and put it in here.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:39.210
It'd be easy to embed this
in any kind of application.

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:41.870
So that's the demo from the
perspective of the API consumer

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.910
or application developer.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.640
Next, let's look at this from
the API product manager's

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:47.510
point of view.

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:51.150
What is it like to
manage this API program?

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:54.180
So let's see how our
API program is running.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.450
To do that, I'm logged
into the Apigee experience.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.777
I'm going to come over and
click on the Analyze button.

00:12:59.777 --> 00:13:01.860
And from there, I see a
bunch of different reports

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:02.520
I can look at.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.020
Let's look at a few
of these and see

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.650
what we can find out about how
the API program is running.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:09.798
This first report shows
us proxy performance,

00:13:09.798 --> 00:13:11.340
and we can see how
much traffic we've

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.892
gotten in the last hour or so.

00:13:13.892 --> 00:13:16.350
And we'll see a few people,
who looks like they're probably

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:17.820
getting rate limited.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:19.590
We see average
response time in here,

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:22.660
we see traffic by proxy and
average response time by proxy.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.140
So this is a good overall
operational metric,

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:28.930
just telling us how our
API program is running.

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:30.810
So this report shows
developer engagement.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.310
What we're seeing
in here is there's

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:36.150
a total of 10 developers, 7 of
them have apps, 4 are active,

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:38.190
and we've got 1
that's highly active.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:42.370
So this gives us an idea of
how engaged our developers are.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:44.620
We can see which devices
people are using.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.530
This might influence how we
support different clients

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:48.920
and things like that.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:51.460
So we can see who's on
mobile, who's on desktop,

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:54.610
and then what kind of
browsers they're using.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:57.470
The GeoMap shows us where
people are calling from.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:01.350
So if we click in and zoom
in on the United States here,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.750
we can see that there's a lot
of traffic from California

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:07.840
and some is coming
from Colorado.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:11.180
This is a custom report that
shows us order status format.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:15.730
So this API will return data
in XML format or JSON format,

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:18.670
depending on what type of
accept header you send.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.790
So if you put application
JSON as the accept type,

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:23.410
it'll return JSON data.

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.180
If you put application
XML in the header,

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.410
it'll return XML data.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:29.000
And that was to support
some older clients.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.550
It's interesting, from the
company's point of view,

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.170
to see how people
are using this.

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:35.630
The XML interface is
a legacy interface,

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:37.380
and we want to see
who's still using that.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:40.208
So this gives us an idea over
time of who's calling what

00:14:40.208 --> 00:14:41.750
and what they're
doing with it, so we

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:43.292
get an idea of what
kind of responses

00:14:43.292 --> 00:14:44.870
people are interested in.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.700
And we could look at
that trend over time.

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:48.110
And it'll show us--

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:49.820
we can compare that
to a previous period.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:56.908
So this'll look at the
previous hour versus this hour.

00:14:56.908 --> 00:14:58.200
We can compare the two periods.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.380
So you can build custom
reports in Apigee

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:04.650
to do anything you want.

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.457
You can grab data
that Apigee stores,

00:15:06.457 --> 00:15:08.790
and you can grab anything out
of the request or response

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.880
and build your own
custom report for that.

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:12.475
So using these
analytics reports,

00:15:12.475 --> 00:15:13.850
building your own
custom reports,

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:15.392
gives you some good
insights into how

00:15:15.392 --> 00:15:19.192
your API program is running.

00:15:19.192 --> 00:15:20.150
GREG BRAIL: OK, thanks.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:21.740
So what Keith showed
you was not only

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:24.770
what does an API look like
as the consumer of an API,

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:27.620
but what does the API
product manager actually do?

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.240
How does that API
product manager

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:32.168
keep track of who's using
the API, and control that.

00:15:32.168 --> 00:15:34.460
So now I want to talk a little
bit about the technology

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:35.918
under the covers,
and what are some

00:15:35.918 --> 00:15:37.400
of the technical
considerations you

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:38.990
have to put into
place if you want

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:42.810
to successfully offer an API
for lots and lots of people.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:45.530
So it all starts with
digital consumer management,

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:48.380
or really the management of
who is consuming your API.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.730
You could just call
this API management.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:53.750
And what we find in the
industry is that a lot of people

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.970
know the terms API management,
and a lot of people

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:59.300
know the terms API gateway, and
they use them interchangeably.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:01.820
And actually, when we
talk about API management,

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:04.370
we're talking about this
thing here that Apigee

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.370
has used many times in the past
that we call the digital value

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:08.090
chain.

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:10.940
But the really important thing
about this from our perspective

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:13.550
is that this is about
managing the relationship

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.280
between the API producers
over there on the right who

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:19.350
are creating that API, those
product managers, architects,

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.200
engineers who have
created the API.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:23.460
And then over to the
left of the API consumer,

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:26.720
that's the actual developer who
is building an app, otherwise

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:30.350
known as a digital experience,
that's going to use that API.

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:32.090
So API consumer
management is all

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:33.990
about managing
that relationship.

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:35.660
So what Keith showed
you in the demo was

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:38.000
he showed you both
the API consumer

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:41.370
side of that relationship as
well as the API producer side.

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:44.510
What does it mean to actually
be a person who manages the API?

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.100
And then later on,
in the next demo,

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:47.720
we'll get into
the details of how

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:50.420
do the API consumers actually
use an API management

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:54.013
product to make all of
those things happen.

00:16:54.013 --> 00:16:55.680
I'll talk about a few
other technologies

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.880
that are really, really
important here as well.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.692
One of them is OAuth.

00:16:59.692 --> 00:17:01.650
OAuth is-- I know we're
jumping into technology

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:04.230
all of a sudden, and this
might seem a little abrupt.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.510
But this is a really important
part of API management

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.910
because what's happened in
the industry over the last six

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:12.180
or seven years is that OAuth
has become the standard for API

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:12.839
authentication.

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:14.760
It's not just the
de facto standard.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.010
It's an actual set
of IETF standards,

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:18.569
which makes it a real standard.

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:20.069
And it's used by
almost everybody.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:23.819
Whenever you are using Google
or Facebook or Twitter or,

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:26.550
maybe not Amazon, but
almost everything out there.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.340
Whenever you get to one of
those pages that says sign in

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:32.460
or any time you do sign in with
Google, sign in with Facebook,

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:34.950
sign in with GitHub,
you're using OAuth.

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.740
OAuth was originally
designed to solve a browser

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:39.192
single sign on problem.

00:17:39.192 --> 00:17:40.650
But OAuth, since
then, has actually

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:43.740
been used very often for
mobile authentication

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:45.900
and for authentication
of all kinds of devices.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.420
And the reason it's important
is because at the end

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:51.480
of the whole OAuth process,
what the device ends up

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.840
with is a token, which
is a bit of stuff that

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:56.730
may have some structure
to it or might just

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.680
be a set of random numbers.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:01.020
Some sort of gateway,
which I have down here

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:02.940
on the bottom, an
API gateway, needs

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:04.440
to actually
authenticate and decide

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:06.450
whether or not to allow
access to the API.

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:09.000
And then how that
token is produced

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:12.250
can now vary depending on
the business requirements.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:16.200
So for instance, it can be as
simple as popping open a form

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.300
where you type in your
username and password.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.730
Or it could involve two
factor authentication,

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.610
physical security
tokens, consent screens--

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.860
those screens we all know
and love that say, hey,

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:28.620
do you give application
x the permission

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:32.340
to see your email address and
look at all of your friends?

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:35.400
That's all the OAuth
protocol and related aspects

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.700
like OpenID Connect make
all of that stuff happen.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:40.380
The important part
about this is that we

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.340
have a couple of parts
of OAuth that are there.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:45.060
One of them is the API gateway,
which actually enforces, where

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.070
you have to have a
proper token in order

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:48.917
to access these services.

00:18:48.917 --> 00:18:51.000
Another is what's called
the authorization server.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.700
Products like Apigee can act
as an authorization server,

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:54.950
but so can many others.

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:57.330
And then API management
is a really important part

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:59.760
of OAuth-- because what
technologists don't understand

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:03.630
right away is that OAuth is not
only end user authentication,

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.520
it's Greg using my API.

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:08.310
It's also application
authentication.

00:19:08.310 --> 00:19:12.540
Greg is making an API call
from version 7 of the iPhone

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:17.340
app for the package tracking API
and for the API products named

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.420
package tracker.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.280
That allows the
API product manager

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:21.780
to know who is using the API.

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:24.240
And it allows the people
responsible for security

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.640
to know things
like, an popped up

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.400
that we didn't know about that's
creating a lot of traffic.

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:32.200
Or we accidentally pushed
out a bad release of the app.

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:33.520
It's got a problem.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:35.640
You have to take all the
API calls from that app

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:38.010
and wrap them and send
them off somewhere else.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.710
Or worst case
scenario, a developer

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:42.300
signed up for the API.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:43.980
They're using the
API in a way that's

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:46.320
inconsistent with
our terms of service.

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:50.167
We have to stop that application
from using our API right away.

00:19:50.167 --> 00:19:51.750
These are all things
that happen a lot

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.190
in the world of API management.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.500
And they're possible because
the OAuth authorization

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:59.650
server is very tightly coupled
with the API management system.

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:03.480
Another thing that happens a lot
on APIs is the idea of a quota.

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:07.320
Quotas were part of the
very first public web APIs.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:09.360
And they exist because
if you're going

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:12.180
to let people you don't
trust use your API,

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.950
you have to have some way to
control how much they use it.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.130
Otherwise, they can
cause big, big production

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:18.480
problems for you.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:20.130
You don't want to
be in a situation

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.310
where someone signed up for your
API, and if you have no OAuth,

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.980
you might not know what
application is using the API.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:27.810
But even if you do,
without a quota,

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:30.990
you don't want them to drive
API usage to such a point

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:32.610
that it produces
denial of service

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.380
for your other customers.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:37.350
Now there is a whole range of
traffic management strategies

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.680
you have to put in place when
you put an API on the Internet.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.810
DDOS, for instance,
the kind of classic TCP

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:44.640
distributed denial
of service attacks

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:47.370
that we all have heard
about from botnets.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:49.590
Those actually, to
a certain degree,

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.320
have to be handled at the
network level, at the IP level.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:54.970
And the big Cloud
providers, for instance,

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:56.730
have sophisticated
technology for this,

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:59.460
as does anyone who runs a lot
of volume on the internet.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:00.840
Similarly, we have
to have things

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:03.660
like rate limits in
routers and gateways

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:05.892
that control you know,
hey, this bit of technology

00:21:05.892 --> 00:21:07.350
really is going to
fall apart if it

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:10.200
handles more than 10,000
concurrent requests.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.090
The quota is more
of a business limit.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:15.390
It controls which application
is allowed to use the API

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:16.470
and how much.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.990
Without a quota, you can't
really have self-service.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.740
You can't let just anyone
sign up for your API

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:23.490
if you can't control
how much they use it.

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:27.120
And it's not only because people
might do nefarious things,

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:29.640
but also because you need
to protect against people

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.320
making dumb mistakes.

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.560
Hey, someone accidentally
pushed out a version of the API.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.650
Whenever it sees an error,
it retries immediately

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:38.760
with no delay.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:40.530
Great, if you have
a quota, at least

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:42.720
you can protect yourself
from that kind of API,

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:44.520
from that sort of
bad access pattern.

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:46.740
Similarly, a quota also
makes it possible to have

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.200
business relationships, to have
different tiers of service.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.910
Keith's API, for
instance, he might

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:54.230
want to say that for free,
you get a very low quota.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:56.300
But if you're a giant
website and you're

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:59.670
going to be driving thousands
of requests a second to the API,

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:02.790
you need a quota so that we can
control how much you use it.

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:06.860
APIs also face all kinds of
interesting security threats.

00:22:06.860 --> 00:22:08.940
Many of these are things
that are familiar to you

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:10.940
if you've done anything
else on the web.

00:22:10.940 --> 00:22:12.710
But there are some
things that might

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:15.420
be a little bit unique to API,
so we need to think about.

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.440
So for instance, if
you're opening an API up

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:21.140
to a third party and you're
letting your third party post

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:23.585
JSON or XML or
anything else to you,

00:22:23.585 --> 00:22:26.210
you're basically letting anyone
who can sign up on the internet

00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:28.610
send arbitrary content
to your server.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:31.760
Now depending on what
technology you have behind it

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:33.668
and what the format
is, that data

00:22:33.668 --> 00:22:35.210
that can be sent to
your server could

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:36.560
contain all kinds of things.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:38.690
In theory, it could contain
things like viruses,

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:40.310
but more likely,
someone might try

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:43.400
to do something like send
you a JSON document that's

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:45.050
nested a million levels deep.

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:47.720
Or send you a JSON document
with a string in it

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:49.250
that's 100 megabytes long.

00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:52.130
And these kinds of things
could actually cause problems

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:54.440
to your back ends, depending
on what kind of technology

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:56.720
stack they implement,
and how robust they are.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:00.230
API gateways like Apigee do
have efficient ways to inject

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:02.450
those kinds of things
before they get in.

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:05.510
There's other things too,
things like SQL injection,

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:06.380
things like--

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:08.990
Google has a service called
data loss prevention, which

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.198
can look at the responses
to your API calls

00:23:11.198 --> 00:23:13.490
and make sure that you're
not inadvertently sending out

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:16.520
people's social security
numbers or credit card numbers

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:17.700
or things like that.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:19.190
So there's a whole
lot of threats

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:21.950
that APIs face, some of
which are specific to APIs.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:25.285
And that, we want to make sure
we address in the API as well.

00:23:25.285 --> 00:23:26.660
And one more thing
that comes up,

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:28.700
it's not necessary
for every API,

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:31.250
but it becomes very important
in the world of APIs,

00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:33.860
is the concept of mediation
and transformation.

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:35.600
And this is where
so many companies--

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:37.550
we started out earlier
on with a slide

00:23:37.550 --> 00:23:39.890
where we showed that people
progress from a simple point

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:41.967
to point integration
scenario to something

00:23:41.967 --> 00:23:43.550
that was more
sophisticated where they

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.260
were building a new platform.

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:47.900
But almost everyone who starts
out in the world of APIs

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:49.670
has some sort of
technology already.

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:51.095
And if you're in
the kind of shop

00:23:51.095 --> 00:23:54.260
where you can rewrite everything
and you could make changes

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:56.780
and you have enough
flexibility in your tech stack,

00:23:56.780 --> 00:23:57.500
that's awesome.

00:23:57.500 --> 00:23:59.885
You could do whatever you
need when you need to do it.

00:23:59.885 --> 00:24:01.760
But people who have
built up their businesses

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.710
over a period of
years, typically

00:24:03.710 --> 00:24:05.630
don't always have that
kind of flexibility

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:07.590
everywhere on their tech stack.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:09.560
So it can sometimes be
very important to be

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:12.500
able to layer an API in
front of other services.

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:14.870
And those services
may vary in how close

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:17.480
they are to the API you
actually want to offer.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.910
Many businesses have
mainframes, and those mainframes

00:24:19.910 --> 00:24:21.620
aren't going away any time soon.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:24.050
[INAUDIBLE] has SAP,
Salesforce, they

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:25.850
have SOAP web services
that were built

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:27.680
in the heyday of the SOAP era.

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:30.620
They have Node JS apps that
might speak very, very well

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:33.680
structured, beautifully
structured APIs, and JSON,

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:34.730
it might just be a mess.

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:37.280
The API gateway is
often used in order

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:40.850
to sort of mediate those legacy
systems and transform things.

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:41.420
Transform

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:45.290
JSON into XML, transform
JSON into GRPC,

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:47.750
transform JSON into a
format that might be easier

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:49.010
to parse for the app.

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:52.980
An API gateway can perform a
very important use case there.

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:54.690
Another thing that
API gateways do,

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.270
which is actually
even more common,

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:58.728
is they act as a
virtualization layer.

00:24:58.728 --> 00:25:00.770
So if I look on my lower
right hand corner there,

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:02.480
we have a collection
of microservices.

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:04.460
And if you're doing
microservices the way

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:08.180
that people who are really
into microservices want to do,

00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:10.940
the whole idea is that that
collection of microservices

00:25:10.940 --> 00:25:14.690
could have five microservices
or 50 services or 5,000

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:15.620
microservices.

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:18.560
And it really shouldn't matter
to the consumer of the API

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:19.370
how that's done.

00:25:19.370 --> 00:25:21.020
The whole point of
microservices is

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:22.880
that the teams that
own those services are

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:26.030
free to rearrange them and
change them and implement them

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:27.890
on the technology
base they want.

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:30.020
And they should be able to
do that without everyone

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:30.780
knowing about it.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:33.140
So it would be a big
disaster if your system made

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:35.630
of 50 microservices was
structured in such a way

00:25:35.630 --> 00:25:38.060
that all of your
customer's API clients

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:40.910
had to change when you wanted
to split that up to now

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:42.870
100 different microservices.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.140
So one very important
things API gateways

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:47.480
do in microservices
architecture is they

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:49.100
act as a virtualization point.

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:51.530
They present a
consistent interface,

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:56.270
a set of HTTP, URIs, and verbs,
your URI patterns and verbs

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:59.600
more specifically, that are
consistent and stable and

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:02.990
well-documented and presented
to that end user application.

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:05.450
And then that API gateway,
behind the covers,

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:09.080
can change as the actual
technology implementation

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:11.060
changes, maybe as
legacy systems are

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.160
replaced with real
systems or new systems.

00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:15.740
In an addition, when
microservices or environments

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.020
are refactored, that
API gateway can also

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:20.000
act as that
virtualization layer.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:21.800
And with that said, I
want to talk quickly

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:25.460
about Google's API management
and API gateway products.

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:28.340
Then we're going to
get into one more demo.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.920
Google, in the world of APIs,
if you go to the Google Cloud

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:33.860
Console, you'll actually see
that we have two major product

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:34.885
families.

00:26:34.885 --> 00:26:37.010
Right now we have a product
called Cloud Endpoints.

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:38.660
Cloud Endpoints
is an API gateway,

00:26:38.660 --> 00:26:40.823
and it's only an API gateway.

00:26:40.823 --> 00:26:43.490
The advantage of Cloud Endpoints
is that it's small, it's cheap,

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:46.340
and it's very well
suited for applications

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:49.100
where you have very basic
needs for an API gateway

00:26:49.100 --> 00:26:51.620
that you can deploy quickly,
especially to Google Cloud.

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:54.000
Google Cloud Endpoints
will run anywhere.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.970
Cloud Endpoints can interact
with some of the other API

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:59.370
infrastructure already
built into Google Cloud,

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:02.460
so you can authenticate an
API call with an API key,

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:03.240
for instance.

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:05.375
Or you can use a
service account token,

00:27:05.375 --> 00:27:07.750
which if you've work with GCP,
you're very familiar with.

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:10.080
And it's great for
those use cases.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:11.250
We also have Apigee.

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:14.310
And Keith and I spent most
of our time on Apigee.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:16.950
Apigee is a full lifecycle
API management product.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:19.890
It's not only a gateway that can
do the various simple gateway

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.350
things that Endpoints
can do, as well as

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:24.300
a more sophisticated
gateway that can even

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:26.550
do some of the transformation
and mediation things

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:27.600
I talked about.

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:30.660
It's also got attached to
it a whole API management

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:33.990
platform that allows API product
managers and others to keep

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:36.150
track of what API
products they've created,

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.370
tie them to documentation.

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:41.160
Control who's actually
allowed to use those products,

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:43.650
what they're allowed to
do, set individual quotas

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:44.910
for different consumers.

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:48.300
Use role-based access control to
control what kind of consumers

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.940
can see which APIs, which
API calls they can make,

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:52.350
what talks they can see.

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:55.710
It's [INAUDIBLE] real designed
for the complete enterprise

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.930
lifecycle of an API program.

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.580
And that is, I
think in my opinion,

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:02.760
equally relevant if you're
offering an API on the internet

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:04.770
to third party
developers as it is

00:28:04.770 --> 00:28:06.750
if you're a large
or even medium sized

00:28:06.750 --> 00:28:10.350
organization that wants to offer
internal APIs that are consumed

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:12.300
by lots of different teams.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:14.550
And this is actually a
picture of that platform.

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:16.050
We talk about some
of the components

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:18.210
of the platform including
developer portals, which

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:22.167
are sort of the key user
facing presence for your API,

00:28:22.167 --> 00:28:24.000
as well as some of the
interesting analytics

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:26.245
we can give that we showed
you earlier in the demo.

00:28:26.245 --> 00:28:27.870
As well as a lot of
services, including

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:30.360
transformation,
security, orchestration,

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:32.450
and those kinds of things.

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:34.745
One other thing I will
mention about Apigee

00:28:34.745 --> 00:28:37.120
is that Apigee actually has
a couple different deployment

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:37.620
options.

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:39.700
This is a very frequently
asked question,

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.250
so we like to talk about
it in the beginning.

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:44.590
The Apigee Edge platform
runs as software

00:28:44.590 --> 00:28:46.840
as a service as part
of Google Cloud, which

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:50.050
means that the API gateway
runs inside Google Cloud.

00:28:50.050 --> 00:28:52.990
It can sit in between clients
and servers really anywhere.

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:54.740
And the whole API
management platform

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:56.530
is run by Google Cloud.

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:58.607
However, we offer
other options as well.

00:28:58.607 --> 00:29:00.190
For instance, there
is a hybrid option

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:02.440
in which you're
allowed to have parts

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:04.900
of the Apigee gateway
running in your data center

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:06.970
with the rest of Apigee
running in Cloud.

00:29:06.970 --> 00:29:08.560
And there's an on
premises option,

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:12.850
where everything runs in your
own space managed by you.

00:29:12.850 --> 00:29:15.190
And that can mean your
own physical data center,

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:18.700
your own Amazon Web Services
account, your own Google Cloud

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:20.320
account, your own Azure account.

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.750
And we've had customers do
all of those kinds of things.

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:25.385
So with that said,
we're actually

00:29:25.385 --> 00:29:27.010
going to go back to
the very last demo.

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:29.650
Keith is going to show us
some of the details of Apigee

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:32.290
and how to use it to
actually add API management

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:34.550
to an existing service.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.040
KEITH DANEKIND: So we saw
how people call the API,

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:39.500
and we saw how people
manage the API program,

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:42.060
and how they build programs
and things like that.

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:44.180
But how do we get the
service up and running?

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:47.800
What do we do in Apigee
to make this work?

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:49.600
So the order service
is a cloud function

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:50.850
that's running a Google Cloud.

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:52.320
And in order to
use it, you've got

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:53.970
to send in an
authorization header.

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:57.360
And the credentials for that
are stored inside of Apigee.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:59.970
On the left hand side of this,
there's a single page web

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:01.290
application that you saw.

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:03.588
It has an API key that
was generated by Apigee.

00:30:03.588 --> 00:30:05.880
And there's a few more mobile
applications, all of them

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:07.470
have their own API key.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:09.930
That lets me track
who's calling.

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:11.500
And it does something else.

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:13.440
There's a few
fields that show up

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:15.600
if you're using an
internal API key, that

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:18.540
don't show up if you're
using an external API key.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:20.707
And the idea is people
inside the company

00:30:20.707 --> 00:30:23.040
might need to know who the
sales team was that fulfilled

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:24.540
this order, but
that's not something

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:26.760
you want to share with
the outside people.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:28.440
So when this runs
through Apigee,

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:30.167
if you're using a
particular API key,

00:30:30.167 --> 00:30:31.500
it will remove that information.

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:33.000
And that gives the
right information

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:33.840
to the right people.

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:37.110
So one URL for everybody,
and different API products

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:39.240
to manage who can see what.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:41.790
So the idea was, we want to
build a facade in the back end.

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:43.440
The fact that this
is a cloud function

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:45.060
or what's running
back there really

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:47.642
isn't that important
to the experience.

00:30:47.642 --> 00:30:49.600
And we want to control
access to some features.

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:51.517
So some of that data,
we might want to remove.

00:30:51.517 --> 00:30:53.017
And they only get
a small portion

00:30:53.017 --> 00:30:55.350
of what might be available
through a real order tracking

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:57.683
system or through a whole
ERP system back there.

00:30:57.683 --> 00:30:59.100
We want to put
some rate limits on

00:30:59.100 --> 00:31:01.350
there so that people don't
call this excessively,

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:02.400
and impose some quotas.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.980
Internal people can call it
a certain number of times,

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.480
and external people also
have their own quotas.

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:08.460
We want to add caching.

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:09.980
So I put a 5 minute
cache on here.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:11.730
Somebody checks the
order again and again,

00:31:11.730 --> 00:31:13.560
it'll just grab
that from memory.

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:15.768
And there's a five minute
time to live on that cache.

00:31:15.768 --> 00:31:18.393
I wanted to reformat the request
and the response a little bit.

00:31:18.393 --> 00:31:20.070
It's mostly good, but
I wanted to remove

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:21.060
a few fields in there.

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.310
So those are different things
you might do with Apigee

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:24.730
in a situation like this.

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:27.240
So let's look into
what I built in here.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:29.000
So when I went to
build the API, I

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:31.190
started off with an
existing service.

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:33.307
So this is just a cloud
function that I can call.

00:31:33.307 --> 00:31:35.015
And we'll take a look
at what it returns.

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:39.750
So we hit the URL, we
put the order number

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:42.840
on the end of there, and we get
back some basic data in here

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:46.230
that shows us about the order.

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:46.890
So that's nice.

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:49.650
And I wanted to control
access to this through Apigee.

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:52.500
And I also wanted to remove
this sales group field,

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:54.700
depending on whether you're
internal or external.

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:56.650
And I also wanted
to support XML.

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:58.770
The internal service
returns JSON,

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:01.560
but some external consumers
are still using XML

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:04.300
and they wanted to get
data in XML format.

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:06.000
So here's the API,
and this is what

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:09.870
it would look like if we
consumed it through Apigee.

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:14.070
So what we'll notice in here
is that field has disappeared.

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:16.890
And we can request XML or JSON.

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:18.630
So in this case, it's JSON.

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:25.670
But if I send a different header
in there, we get back XML.

00:32:25.670 --> 00:32:26.750
There it is formatted.

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:29.482
So, same data,
but in XML format.

00:32:29.482 --> 00:32:31.690
And that's going to support
clients who are currently

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:34.397
living in the XML world.

00:32:34.397 --> 00:32:35.980
So other things we
could do with this.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:37.990
I've got two different API keys.

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:40.420
I'm sending API key
as a URL parameter.

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:43.180
And depending on which one I
use, I get back an extra field.

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:47.980
So this is the API key that's
intended for external people.

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:50.440
And then if I switch
to this other API key

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:53.110
and make the call
again, we'll notice

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:54.280
that sales group shows up.

00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:55.390
And that was the
field that I wanted

00:32:55.390 --> 00:32:56.740
to hide from outside people.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:58.900
So you can hand out
different API keys.

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:00.400
And this is
personalized, it's going

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.590
to give you the right
response to the right person.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.012
And I'll show how I
did that in Apigee.

00:33:05.012 --> 00:33:06.470
So that's the data
that comes back.

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:08.830
Let's look at the
actual API and what

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:11.680
I had to do to turn this
regular service into something

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:13.602
that I could manage with Apigee.

00:33:13.602 --> 00:33:15.680
So let's look at
the API proxy that I

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:17.190
built to make this happen.

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:19.520
So I've logged into
the Apigee experience,

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:22.160
and I'm going to play the
role of API developer.

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:24.290
So I have that
existing service, I

00:33:24.290 --> 00:33:26.870
want to manage access
to it using Apigee.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:28.550
So I came over to API Proxies.

00:33:31.697 --> 00:33:33.280
From here, you can
create a new proxy.

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:34.430
But what we're
going to do is we'll

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:36.920
look at this border status
proxy and see what's in there.

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:43.578
So there's the back end URL.

00:33:43.578 --> 00:33:45.870
And there's the URL in Apigee
that we're going to call.

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:47.370
So we call Apigee.

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:49.865
It manages this connection,
the request and response flow.

00:33:49.865 --> 00:33:51.240
It's going to send
it on to here,

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:53.380
and then we'll get
the response back.

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:55.755
So we'll click over
here on Develop.

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:05.590
And we'll see within
our flow, we've

00:34:05.590 --> 00:34:06.680
got a few different steps.

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:08.860
So these different policies
do different things.

00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:10.940
You can configure
them to do things.

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:14.929
There's a whole palette
of them right over here.

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:17.260
So let's look at
what I've added.

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:19.429
I've added a verify
API key check.

00:34:19.429 --> 00:34:22.300
So I'm going to
look in the request

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.400
for a query parameter
called API key,

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:26.469
and that's what I'm going
to check for API keys.

00:34:26.469 --> 00:34:28.344
If we don't send one,
we're going to get back

00:34:28.344 --> 00:34:29.980
an unauthorized error.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:32.710
Spike arrest is going to limit
this to 100 calls per minute.

00:34:32.710 --> 00:34:34.293
You can set that to
whatever you like,

00:34:34.293 --> 00:34:36.460
and that's just to
protect our back end.

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:38.469
And then we saw in
the report that I

00:34:38.469 --> 00:34:40.659
was able to tell whether
somebody was requesting

00:34:40.659 --> 00:34:42.429
XML data or JSON data.

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:44.739
I did that with the
statistics collector.

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:47.500
I'm grabbing the
accept header, and I'm

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:49.250
recording that as request type.

00:34:49.250 --> 00:34:52.179
And then I can put that
in my custom report.

00:34:52.179 --> 00:34:53.830
I've got a response
cache in here,

00:34:53.830 --> 00:34:57.610
and the response cache has
a 300 second time to live.

00:34:57.610 --> 00:35:00.710
And then the key has two
different fragments to it.

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:02.867
So I wanted to do
it off of the URI.

00:35:02.867 --> 00:35:04.450
And that worked quite
well, but then I

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:06.912
figured out that I was
caching the wrong type of data

00:35:06.912 --> 00:35:08.120
for the wrong type of person.

00:35:08.120 --> 00:35:11.380
So it's possible that
people send in the same URL,

00:35:11.380 --> 00:35:14.590
but one of them gets XML back
and one of them gets JSON back.

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:17.695
So we also have to look at
the accept type in the header

00:35:17.695 --> 00:35:19.570
to make sure we send
back the right response,

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:22.030
and that it gets
cached correctly.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.400
The back end system requires
an authorization header.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:27.100
I've got the secrets for that
stored in a key value map

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:28.270
inside of Apigee.

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:30.700
So what this will do is it'll
go grab the order status

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:32.800
authentication code
from the key value map

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:34.973
and store it in a variable.

00:35:34.973 --> 00:35:36.640
And then we set that
as an authorization

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:38.640
header that we're going
to send to the back end,

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:40.510
and that lets us in.

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:42.820
On the way back, we're going
to conditionally remove

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:43.540
the sales group.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:45.582
And I've got some JavaScript
in there to do that.

00:35:45.582 --> 00:35:49.180
So this makes a reference
to remove-sales-group.js.

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:51.760
And right down here,
three lines of JavaScript.

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:52.520
Very simple.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:55.420
So we're going to
grab the response

00:35:55.420 --> 00:35:58.510
and turn it into an
object in JavaScript.

00:35:58.510 --> 00:36:00.968
And then we're just going to
delete the field, sales group.

00:36:00.968 --> 00:36:02.635
And then we're going
to take that object

00:36:02.635 --> 00:36:04.000
and turn it back into a string.

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:06.400
And then we're going to
replace the outgoing response

00:36:06.400 --> 00:36:08.665
content with that new,
improved response.

00:36:12.412 --> 00:36:14.370
After that, this will
get called conditionally.

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:17.760
So based on whether they've
had that header or not,

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:19.920
we'll convert it
to XML or we won't.

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:22.410
And then we've got
core support in here

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:25.110
so that we can call
this from the browser.

00:36:25.110 --> 00:36:26.580
So that's our basic API proxy.

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:29.695
Wasn't too bad to build.

00:36:29.695 --> 00:36:31.190
OK, let's do some questions.

00:36:31.190 --> 00:36:33.190
GREG BRAIL: Someone said
restricting application

00:36:33.190 --> 00:36:34.540
to certain APIs.

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:37.720
There's actually multiple
levels of which you might

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:39.310
want access control, right?

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:41.110
If you're using,
for instance, OAuth,

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:44.800
to protect access to an API,
then to a certain degree,

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:46.360
it's up to what
user authentication

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:48.130
system is behind
your OAuth as to what

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:50.200
end users can use your API.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:53.140
But the thing that Apigee,
and any API management tool,

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:55.810
gives you is the
ability to control

00:36:55.810 --> 00:36:57.910
what applications
can use your API,

00:36:57.910 --> 00:36:59.900
and which developers can
build an application.

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:02.380
So you can set
controls on who is

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:05.800
allowed to build an
application for a specific API.

00:37:05.800 --> 00:37:07.630
You can even set
controls in Apigee

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:10.540
on, for instance, who's allowed,
and to see documentation

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:13.690
for API and find out
which APIs exist.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:16.210
There are a lot of things
in there based on things

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:17.800
we've learned about that.

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:18.850
KEITH DANEKIND: Right.

00:37:18.850 --> 00:37:21.520
GREG BRAIL: We always
get the latency question.

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:24.370
And the answer is, it depends.

00:37:24.370 --> 00:37:27.670
When Apigee runs as a
service, the actual proxy

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:29.770
runs in a Google
Cloud data center.

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:31.540
We have a lot of those.

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:33.790
They also happen to be very,
very close to Amazon data

00:37:33.790 --> 00:37:35.590
centers, and very
well connected.

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:40.390
So if your target is in the
same region or very close

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:42.400
or in the same
geographical area,

00:37:42.400 --> 00:37:44.920
then it depends on what
you do with the policies.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:47.650
But it's no more than
a few milliseconds

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:49.150
plus network latency.

00:37:49.150 --> 00:37:52.570
If your data center is on an
aircraft carrier in the South

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.850
Pacific being
connected by satellite,

00:37:54.850 --> 00:37:56.645
then it's going to
have a lot of latency.

00:37:56.645 --> 00:37:57.520
KEITH DANEKIND: Sure.

00:37:57.520 --> 00:38:00.520
And yeah, so the when
I was writing, just

00:38:00.520 --> 00:38:02.790
as an example, this demo,
I had a cloud function

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:03.790
running on the back end.

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:07.480
And I was seeing about 300
millisecond response time.

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:10.600
And that was just network
between me and the data center.

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:13.310
And I saw that cut in half
once I added caching to it.

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:15.820
So if you had caching, you may
find that the performance can

00:38:15.820 --> 00:38:17.737
be drastically increased
because we don't have

00:38:17.737 --> 00:38:19.110
to talk to the back end at all.

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:20.735
So, a few strategies
to deal with that.

00:38:20.735 --> 00:38:21.970
GREG BRAIL: Exactly.

00:38:21.970 --> 00:38:23.565
Someone asked about
Cloud Endpoints.

00:38:23.565 --> 00:38:24.940
I should actually
talk about that

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:26.860
since that's a big
part of my life.

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:28.580
Cloud Endpoints
and Apigee, today,

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:30.920
do different things for
different customers.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:32.620
So for instance,
Cloud Endpoints is

00:38:32.620 --> 00:38:37.420
very handy for sharing an API
with other Google Cloud users,

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:41.290
and runs at a low cost,
is easy to set up,

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:43.450
does a fairly limited
number of things.

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:45.850
Apigee is a full featured
API management product

00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:47.980
with a whole bunch of
complex enterprise features

00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:50.110
like we've been
talking about now.

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:52.450
Over time, you know, both
Cloud Endpoints and Apigee

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:55.430
come from the same company
and the same group of people,

00:38:55.430 --> 00:38:59.170
so over time, we're going to
make those things work together

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:00.340
more seamlessly.

00:39:00.340 --> 00:39:03.040
Exactly what's going to happen
and when is not something

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:04.750
I can talk about right now.

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:06.682
But if you are using
Cloud Endpoints,

00:39:06.682 --> 00:39:08.140
we'd actually love
to hear from you

00:39:08.140 --> 00:39:11.350
as well because we're trying
to get some customer feedback.

00:39:11.350 --> 00:39:13.382
Again, right now, they
serve different purposes

00:39:13.382 --> 00:39:14.590
for different kinds of users.

00:39:14.590 --> 00:39:17.162
And we want to make sure
that we respect all that.

00:39:17.162 --> 00:39:18.370
KEITH DANEKIND: Thanks, Greg.

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:19.510
GREG BRAIL: There's
a question here.

00:39:19.510 --> 00:39:22.010
The person is asking, we worked
in the past with a two layer

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:23.920
model where we
had an API gateway

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:27.550
on the edge communicating to
an internal API gateway that

00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:31.077
did mediation,
composition, and so on.

00:39:31.077 --> 00:39:32.410
Where does Apigee fit into this?

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:35.090
So is Apigee-- is it OK to
put these two things together?

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:38.212
So you could
certainly use Apigee

00:39:38.212 --> 00:39:39.670
as a simple gateway
on the outside.

00:39:39.670 --> 00:39:41.503
It can also do a lot
of this mediation stuff

00:39:41.503 --> 00:39:43.210
so you saw when I
use a JavaScript

00:39:43.210 --> 00:39:46.030
policy in the demo in there to
change the request to response,

00:39:46.030 --> 00:39:47.260
do dynamic routing.

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:48.910
A lot of that can
be done in Apigee.

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.640
And it's really an architectural
choice about where you do that

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:53.945
and how that fits into
your organization.

00:39:53.945 --> 00:39:56.320
People like that they can
quickly build things in Apigee.

00:39:56.320 --> 00:39:59.020
I would say if you've
got full blown business

00:39:59.020 --> 00:40:01.300
logic that's long
running and complicated,

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:02.470
put that behind Apigee.

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:03.610
If you've got
something that's going

00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:05.140
to be stateless
and dynamic, that's

00:40:05.140 --> 00:40:07.810
related to returning the right
format or something like that,

00:40:07.810 --> 00:40:09.310
that's great to do in Apigee.

00:40:09.310 --> 00:40:11.320
KEITH DANEKIND: Someone is
asking about baseline security

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:11.830
controls.

00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:13.413
Do you have baseline
security controls

00:40:13.413 --> 00:40:15.910
developed for Apigee
and VSI and kubernetes?

00:40:15.910 --> 00:40:17.860
I don't know what
VSI is, but I will

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:19.973
say that we have a
security reporting thing

00:40:19.973 --> 00:40:20.890
that we're working on.

00:40:20.890 --> 00:40:22.523
I actually know--
I think it's alpha.

00:40:22.523 --> 00:40:24.190
GREG BRAIL: Yeah,
it'll do a report on--

00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:24.820
KEITH DANEKIND: But, I mean--

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:25.480
GREG BRAIL: --what
you've implemented.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:27.475
KEITH DANEKIND: In terms
of your use of Apigee,

00:40:27.475 --> 00:40:30.100
if you're a customer, we'd love
to talk to you more about that.

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:32.080
Because we're
looking at basically,

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:34.420
how do we tell you as
an Apigee customer,

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:37.000
you know, hey, you have security
policies about use of TLS

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:39.125
and things like that, and
you're not following them

00:40:39.125 --> 00:40:40.930
in this particular API proxy.

00:40:40.930 --> 00:40:43.540
And you can use that to help
make sure those things are

00:40:43.540 --> 00:40:44.347
being enforced.

00:40:44.347 --> 00:40:45.430
GREG BRAIL: Great, thanks.

00:40:45.430 --> 00:40:47.853
So that's all the time we've
got for questions today.

00:40:47.853 --> 00:40:50.020
I wanted to remind you
that-- speaking of security--

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:52.360
there's a Gartner Report
in the Resources tab,

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:54.850
and it's entitled How to Build
an Effective API Security

00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:55.630
Strategy.

00:40:55.630 --> 00:40:57.940
And you can download that
through the resources window

00:40:57.940 --> 00:40:59.750
or on your screen.

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:01.593
Thank you so much
for attending today.

00:41:01.593 --> 00:41:03.010
We'll see you in
the next webcast.

00:41:03.010 --> 00:41:06.060
